@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+## Why
+
+`change` in `src/domain/list.ts` reads the clock before the update runs, so a failed complete, reopen or tag (`invalid-state`, `invalid-input`) still consumes a clock reading. The documented contract is one reading per successful change; a sequence-based clock gets timestamps shifted by every failed operation.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- The list reads the clock only once the update is known to succeed.
+- Tests check that failed changes do not read the clock.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `task-timestamps`: the clock is read once per successful change, and not at all for a failed one.
+
+## Impact
+
+- Rules touched: no wall clock in the domain (the clock stays caller-supplied), errors returned as `Result` (unchanged), tests for the behaviour change. No dependency added.
+- Public API in `src/index.ts` does not change.
+- Code: `src/domain/list.ts`; tests in `test/`.
